# Changelog and documentation updates

When you complete work that changes **user-visible behavior**, **public APIs**, **CLI flags**, **configuration**, **installation**, **HTTP contracts**, **UI flows**, or **anything already described** under `docs/`, treat documentation and the changelog as part of the same change — not optional follow-up.

## Changelog

- Edit the repo-root **`CHANGELOG.md`**.
- Add entries under **`## [Unreleased]`** using the existing sections (**Added**, **Changed**, **Deprecated**, **Removed**, **Fixed**, **Security**) and tone: concise bullets, module paths or symbols in backticks where helpful.
- If the change only touches the **`ui/`** package, also update **`ui/CHANGELOG.md`** when that file already documents the UI release stream.

## Documentation (`docs/`)

- Update the **smallest set of Markdown pages** that would otherwise become wrong or incomplete (for example `docs/http-api.md`, `docs/install.md`, how-to guides, UI pages under `docs/ui/`, concept pages under `docs/concepts/`).
- If navigation or the site map should change, update **`mkdocs.yml`** (nav) in the same change.
- Keep terminology aligned with **`docs/glossary.md`** when you introduce or rename user-facing terms.

## When you may skip

- Purely internal refactors, comments, or tests with **no** observable or documented contract change.
- Typo-only fixes in code comments or non-user docs **unless** they correct a misleading public name or API.

If unsure whether users or integrators would care, **update the changelog and docs anyway**; favor clarity over leaving stale docs.
